00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.810
Okay, now back to this
incredibly important story

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.243
on how Europe stole the world.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.130
Yes, I'm gonna be looking at a lot of maps

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.330
to understand this issue

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:00.753
because drawing on the map
is how they did things.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.810
(dramatic music)

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.280
To understand how Europe stole the world,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.070
we need to rewind to
back when the map looked

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.723
a little different, hold on.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:22.353
Back in like 1450, the
world map looked like this.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.910
I mean, Europe was just a
bunch of smaller kingdoms.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.250
The borders look so
different than they do today.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.410
They were all pretty small potatoes.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.020
Well actually there
actually were no potatoes.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.060
Those wouldn't arrive to
Europe for another 50 years

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.280
but the fact is there wasn't an abundance

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.590
of anything in Europe.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.640
Instead, Europeans were just
farmers barely scraping by,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.640
constantly in debt to
a few rich landlords.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.040
Life in Europe was scarce and miserable.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.380
Meanwhile, there was a
bunch of other empires

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.540
around the world that were thriving.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.150
There was this big empire
over in the Middle East.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.250
They had silk and then over here in India,

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.990
you had a big empire with spices

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.270
and luxurious cotton fabrics.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.910
Then of course there was
the big dynasty in China

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.270
that had by this time already
invented gun powder, porcelain

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.130
and really sophisticated ships,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.530
that they could use to
sail around the world.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.830
Oh, and all of these empires
were trading with one another,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.080
making all of them even richer.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.800
They all had better
everything than Europe did

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.680
and miserable Europeans
eventually wanted in

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.668
on all the trade.

00:05:26.668 --> 00:05:29.520
(funky music)

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.010
So that's kind of the context
here that sets the stage

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.510
for Europe taking over the world.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.570
The first two countries that
wanted in on all this trade

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.180
and set out to explore were
the countries that today

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.950
we know is Spain and Portugal.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.550
So they start looking for a
land route to get them east.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.500
They start moving east and nope.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.360
Oh, look, it's the Ottoman Empire,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.910
a very powerful Middle Eastern empire

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.440
that's run by Muslims.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.080
Meanwhile, Spain and
Portugal are hella Catholic

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.960
and they're not being super
nice to Muslims or Jews.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.157
So the Ottomans are like,
"No, we're not gonna let you

00:06:00.157 --> 00:06:03.697
"pass through our land to get
in on the trade in the east.

00:06:03.697 --> 00:06:06.277
"Oh and by the way, you
can't even afford our taxes.

00:06:06.277 --> 00:06:08.407
"You're miserable Europeans
without a lot of money,

00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:10.080
"so go away."

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.933
They're gonna need another way to go east.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.290
Portugal's work around was literally to go

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.663
around the continent of Africa.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.320
Stopping along the coast to trade

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.230
with the wealthy African
empires along the way.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.890
This is a huge moment
because it actually worked.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.960
It's near the end of the 1400s

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.750
and the Portuguese are now going nuts.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.640
They're sending ships around this route

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.190
that they figured out,
trading with the east

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.080
and suddenly the Portuguese are dressing

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.060
in luxurious fabrics.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.480
They're using cloves and
cinnamon and black pepper

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.580
to make their food actually taste good.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:51.413
Wait a minute, you're telling me

00:06:51.413 --> 00:06:54.150
they didn't have black pepper before this?

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.370
Geez, miserable indeed.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.660
- You must have some peppers.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:00.120
- So the age of exploration has begun.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.360
Miserable Europe is doing
something about their situation.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.380
Going out to get in on the
thriving trade in the east.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.800
But remember, this isn't
about conquering land.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.441
It's about trading, for now.

00:07:14.441 --> 00:07:17.191
(dramatic music)

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.040
Okay, so now Spain sees
Portugal hitting the jackpot

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.620
and they're kind of jelly,
they want in on this too.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.890
They need to establish their
own trade route to Asia

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.650
but they wanted to do things
a little bit differently.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.180
Instead of following Portugal's lead

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.880
and going around Africa,
they listen to a pitch

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.000
from this Italian sailor
and this sailor wants them

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.570
to fund his new startup
called what if we sailed west

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.080
to get to Asia instead of
east, super promising name.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.290
Now remember at this point,
people in Europe thought

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.080
that the world looked like
this, the logic was like,

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:54.930
oh we can just hop over to
Asia by going this other way.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.980
So Spain was like, yes, great
idea, high risk, high return.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.123
Do it Columbus.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.540
Well, it turns out the world
does not look like this

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.760
and instead over here,
there are a couple of

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.270
massive continents that Columbus ran into.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.160
But like many of us confident white dudes,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.830
Columbus was like, yeah,
I know exactly where I am.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.360
We're like in the Indies right now.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.940
Like over those guys over
there, those are Indians.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.260
Yep, I can feel it.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.990
But instead he was like in Cuba,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.580
but despite the mansplaining,

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.280
Columbus kind of found exactly
what he was looking for

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:30.420
and more.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.090
Great empires with rich culture and food

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.210
and precious metal that weren't
available to him back home,

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.950
he was right on target.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.560
So he's like, "All right,
guys, let's trade."

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.907
But then he is like, "Wait a minute.

00:08:42.907 --> 00:08:45.607
"These people don't have armies or navies

00:08:45.607 --> 00:08:47.550
"protecting their stuff."

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.580
So this must be kind of a different place,

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:53.370
different land with different rules

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.130
and this is where the
biggest light bulb moment

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:59.257
of the century goes
off in this guy's head.

00:08:59.257 --> 00:09:02.317
"Change of plans, boys." says Columbus,

00:09:02.317 --> 00:09:04.500
"We're going to claim this land."

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.817
And everyone was like, "Wait, what?"

00:09:06.817 --> 00:09:09.810
"You heard me, we are claiming this land."

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.467
and the boys are like, "Wait,

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:13.747
"weren't we here to like
trade with great empires.

00:09:13.747 --> 00:09:14.947
"Portugal's been doing this

00:09:14.947 --> 00:09:16.350
"for like all these
years and getting rich."

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.307
and he's like, "No,
they don't have armies.

00:09:18.307 --> 00:09:20.137
"They're just letting us show up

00:09:20.137 --> 00:09:21.907
"and like be here without a fight.

00:09:21.907 --> 00:09:23.317
"So obviously that means they don't mind

00:09:23.317 --> 00:09:25.020
"if we just like claim the land."

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.700
I mean, I'm taking some
creative Liberty here

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.860
on like explaining what
Columbus was thinking.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.563
But like, look at the
letter that he wrote.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.430
Here's the letter that
Columbus wrote back to the king

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:38.400
and queen, reporting on what he saw.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.430
He writes back saying that
he discovered many islands,

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.680
inhabited by numerous peoples

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.410
and then I took possession of them

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.370
for our most fortunate king,
making public proclamation

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.520
and unfurling his standard, his flag,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.760
with no one making any resistance,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.600
no one making any resistance.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.850
Spain and Portugal set out to trade.

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.430
They sent out to like explore
and understand and get in

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:06.630
on the global economy.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.650
But Columbus's realization
was instead of trading,

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:14.160
they could actually just
claim land for themselves

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.050
and this changed everything.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:20.280
After all, no one was
making any resistance.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.290
This moment is the foundation

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:26.220
upon which all European imperialism grows.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.317
I'll show you.

00:10:27.317 --> 00:10:30.067
(dramatic music)

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.880
It's the moment that would
give us the first big division

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.170
in the minds of Europeans.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:39.930
The thing we wrote on paper, the old world

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.490
versus the new world.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:43.830
This is really familiar language to us now

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.630
but it was invented in this moment.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.500
The new world was now seen as
land that could be claimed.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.990
It was fair game to go conquer
because they didn't have

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:53.857
big armies or a Christian King.

00:10:53.857 --> 00:10:56.827
"Forget trade with the
east," said, Columbus,

00:10:56.827 --> 00:10:58.417
"There's a bunch of land that we can go

00:10:58.417 --> 00:11:02.269
"take over in the west."
and the race was on.

00:11:02.269 --> 00:11:05.102
(inspiring music)

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.830
Spain and Portugal didn't
wanna fight with each

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.330
other over all this land,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.910
this conquest that they
were about to undertake.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:14.210
So they got together and
they drew some lines.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:21.060
No, literally they just
drew two straight lines.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:22.470
We have Africa, we have Europe

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:23.610
and then we have this line.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:26.280
Everything left of this line is Spains,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.500
everything to the right is Portugal.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.360
This is literally one of the original maps

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.250
where they actually drew
these straight lines

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.320
and divided the world into two.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:37.023
But let's look at this on a
nicer looking map, please.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.210
There we go, now we can see very clearly.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.550
Oh, look now we understand
why they speak Portuguese

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:45.383
in Brazil.

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:48.690
So Spain and Portugal
now have their spheres

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.460
of the world that they can go conquer.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:51.660
They didn't ask anyone.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.460
They didn't negotiate with the locals.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.380
They just drew these straight lines.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.040
The Pope actually helped them
broker the deal, classic.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.560
But again, this is a huge
thing that I want to explain

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.010
in this series, that
these were not just lines.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.930
They weren't just like lines on paper.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:10.200
They were actually a shift
in the paradigm, a new way

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:14.850
of thinking about the world,
no one making any resistance.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:18.000
It was a story that Europeans
could now tell themselves.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.220
Old world versus new world,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:24.330
claimed versus unclaimed,
a new way of thinking.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:26.730
Think I'm being overly
simplistic about this?

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:27.960
Let me show you something.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.400
(dramatic music)

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:34.113
There's one, two, three, four.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.013
These were a juicy find.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:48.390
I found this amazing
Portuguese nautical Atlas.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:51.240
It's a bunch of maps that the
Portuguese Navy drafted up

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:54.300
right after these two
lines divided the world

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.620
between Spain and Portugal

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:59.460
and it is an amazing series
of maps, but not only

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:01.203
because it's visually stunning,

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:05.730
but also because it lets us into the mind

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.250
of how the Portuguese saw the world

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:09.993
after they divided it with Spain.

00:13:09.993 --> 00:13:12.743
(relaxing music)

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.500
This Atlas reads like a story book.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:24.150
It's like the Portuguese were so giddy

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.970
about giving themselves permission
to steal half the world,

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.950
that they had to write a
children's story about it.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:30.750
That's what it feels like,
that's not what it is,

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:34.020
but just look at this thing,
here's the title page.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.810
You can see that at the
center is the sphere

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.630
that was given to Portuguese.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.680
Brazil, Africa, what
is now the East Indies

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:44.730
and here on every corner,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:48.360
you see these angels that are
blowing wind into the sales

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.573
of the Portuguese who are out
on their land grab mission.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.520
So this is just the title page.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:02.460
The subsequent pages of
this Atlas are closeups

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:04.140
on different regions.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:05.340
This is the Caribbean over here.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:06.900
Look, this will become Cuba.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.560
This will become Haiti, Dominican
Republic, North America,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:14.700
South America, all of this
unclaimed up for grabs.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:17.520
Here, our story depicts all these flags.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.490
Some of them are Spanish flags
on the islands on their side

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.650
of the line, but then
you have Portuguese flags

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.150
on the Portuguese side of the line.

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.590
The status of this land,

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.510
according to the fantasy
of the Portuguese,

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:31.590
changing from unclaimed
to claimed by men on ships

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:34.020
with Christian crosses on their sails,

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.900
showing up and planting flags.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.627
This was like playing
a board game to them.

00:14:39.627 --> 00:14:42.377
(dramatic music)

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:45.450
Here's another page.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:48.030
This one's a close up on Brazil,

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.400
and look, we've got people over here.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.530
We've got dark skinned, naked people

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.083
with primitive tools and no homes.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.480
It looks like down here in Brazil,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:00.750
they spend their days chopping
wood naked and dancing,

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:05.140
all while being gazed upon by
fantastical birds and dragons?

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.980
This was an official Naval
Atlas drafted for the king

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:12.960
and if its message wasn't clear enough

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.210
on how these people should be depicted,

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:19.260
the Atlas has a little info
box up here where they say

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.000
that the Brazilians are quote,
"Savage and very brutal."

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.790
This is one of the first atlases ever made

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:29.550
and it wasn't really a navigation tool.

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.290
It was much more a declaration

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.210
of a new way of seeing the world.

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:36.360
Unclaimed territory, sub-human people,

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:39.390
all of it up for grabs by Christian boats.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.330
So yeah, this is how it all started.

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.230
Under this new paradigm, both
empires expanded very quickly

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.090
forcing the Spanish and
Portuguese languages

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.590
across the ocean,

00:15:49.590 --> 00:15:53.160
mining, vast quantities of
silver, creating plantations

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:54.840
for tobacco and sugar

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:57.780
and in the process, massacring
the people who were living

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:01.200
in this new world, both
with their weapons,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:03.810
but also with their European city germs

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.960
that the people being conquered
didn't have immunity to.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.270
I mean, this whole thing
started with curiosity,

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:12.720
with a thirst to get in
on the trade in Asia.

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.810
But soon as this fantasy of unclaimed land

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:18.570
came into the picture, the goal shifted,

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.900
the project pivoted and soon
Europe was telling themselves

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:25.320
a new story that they,
enlightened Christians,

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.030
were entitled to claim it

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.850
and while the story starts
with Spain and Portugal,

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.560
soon it spread.

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:33.990
Other European countries
poked their head up

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:36.487
from their farm work and said,

00:16:36.487 --> 00:16:40.327
"Hey, we're white Christians
also, we know how to sail.

00:16:40.327 --> 00:16:44.160
"We want in on this
story of unclaimed land."

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:46.290
And that's the next chapter of the story.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:49.353
One that I will tell you in
the next part of the series.

